Drug discovery takes on average 12 years and $2.6 billion per approved drug; AI-assisted discovery pipelines at Insilico Medicine and Recursion have produced clinical candidates in under 18 months.
The WHO estimates 400 million people lack access to basic healthcare; AI-powered diagnostic tools (smartphone dermatology, tuberculosis screening from chest X-rays) can work at the point of care without specialist infrastructure.
AI tools trained on retinal photographs can now predict cardiovascular risk, age, and diabetic status from an eye scan — a non-invasive screening capability that didn't exist five years ago.
Rare disease diagnosis currently takes an average of 4–5 years; AI tools analysing genomic and phenotypic data have reduced diagnostic odysseys to weeks for some patients.
Mental health AI tools (Woebot, Wysa) have demonstrated significant symptom reduction in RCTs — not replacing therapists but providing always-available support at zero marginal cost.
